      "SKILL.md": "---\nname: writing-skills\ndescription: \"Use when creating, adapting, patching, or validating Hermes skills, including importing third-party skill ideas into this profile without duplicating stale or unsafe instructions.\"\nversion: 1.0.0\nauthor: Hermes Agent (adapted from obra/superpowers)\nlicense: MIT\nmetadata:\n  hermes:\n    tags: [skills, authoring, adaptation, validation, procedural-memory]\n    related_skills: [hermes-agent-skill-authoring, hermes-agent, verification-before-completion]\n---\n\n# Writing Skills\n\n## Overview\n\nUse this when turning a successful workflow into reusable procedural memory. Skills should reduce future steering, not become a junk drawer of one-off notes.\n\nCore rule: write skills as operational procedures with triggers, steps, pitfalls, and verification.\n\n## When to Use\n\nUse when:\n\n- Creating a new Hermes skill\n- Importing or adapting third-party skill content\n- Patching an existing skill after discovering a missing step\n- Writing references/templates/scripts under a skill\n- Validating skill frontmatter and trigger quality\n\nFor in-repo Hermes Agent skills, also load `hermes-agent-skill-authoring`. For user-local profile skills, use `skill_manage`.\n\n## Before Creating\n\n1. Search/list existing skills.\n2. Prefer patching an umbrella skill over creating a narrow duplicate.\n3. Create a new skill only when the trigger class is reusable and distinct.\n4. Do not store task progress, PR numbers, issue numbers, or stale facts in skills.\n5. If importing from another project, adapt to Hermes tools and keep license attribution.\n\n## Skill Shape\n\nA good Hermes skill has:\n\n- YAML frontmatter with name and description\n- Clear “When to Use” triggers\n- Actionable workflow steps\n- Pitfalls/common failure modes\n- Verification checklist\n- Related skills in metadata where helpful\n\n## Description Discipline\n\nThe description is the trigger. Make it specific:\n\nGood: “Use when receiving code review feedback…”\n\nBad: “Helps with code.”\n\nDescriptions should say when to use the skill, not merely what it is about.\n\n## Adaptation Rules\n\nWhen adapting third-party skills:\n\n- Keep useful principles, not platform-specific commands that do not exist here\n- Map actions to Hermes tools (`skill_view`, `skill_manage`, `delegate_task`, `todo`, file tools)\n- Remove instructions that conflict with higher-priority user/system rules\n- Preserve attribution and license if derived from external content\n- Narrow global triggers if they would add noise outside coding work\n\n### External Hermes skill installation\n\nBefore installing a community skill, inspect the resolved provenance rather than trusting an `owner/repo/path` string or the upstream install snippet. Registry basename collisions can resolve to a different package, while a direct raw `SKILL.md` URL may install only that file and omit required scripts or assets.\n\nFor a packaged external skill:\n\n1. Run `hermes skills inspect` and verify author, homepage, source, and version.\n2. Stage the named repository and verify the complete skill subtree.\n3. Keep backups outside active skill-discovery roots so they do not become duplicate skills.\n4. Install from the verified source, then compare staged and installed files.\n5. Run deep audit, a side-effect-free preflight/diagnostic, `hermes skills list`, and `skill_view` before claiming success.\n\nSee `references/importing-external-hermes-skills.md` for the full provenance, package-completeness, backup, audit, and verification runbook.\n\n## Validation\n\nBefore claiming a skill was installed or updated:\n\n- Use `skill_manage` result path or read the file back\n- Confirm frontmatter starts at byte 0 with `---`\n- Confirm `name` and `description` exist\n- Confirm description is not overly broad\n- Confirm linked skills exist or are intentionally aspirational\n- Load with `skill_view` if the runtime can see it\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n1. Creating a new skill when a patch to an existing one would be better.\n2. Writing a plan instead of a reusable procedure.\n3. Making the description too vague, so the skill never triggers.\n4. Making the description too broad, so it triggers constantly.\n5. Copying platform-specific instructions without Hermes adaptation.\n6. Forgetting to verify that the skill actually loads.\n\n## Verification Checklist\n\n- [ ] Existing skills checked first\n- [ ] Create vs patch decision justified\n- [ ] Skill has trigger, workflow, pitfalls, verification\n- [ ] External attribution preserved when adapted\n- [ ] Hermes-specific tools and constraints used\n- [ ] File creation/patch verified by tool output\n",
      "references/importing-external-hermes-skills.md": "# Importing External Hermes Skills Safely\n\nUse this procedure when installing a community skill whose repository contains `SKILL.md` plus scripts, references, assets, or templates.\n\n## 1. Resolve provenance before installation\n\nRun `hermes skills inspect <identifier>` and verify the displayed author, homepage, source repository, and version. A GitHub-looking `owner/repo/path` identifier can collide with a registry entry that has the same skill basename. Do not install when the resolved source differs from the repository the user named.\n\nIf resolution collides, inspect/install through the repository's direct raw `SKILL.md` URL. Treat the URL as provenance selection, not proof that the full package will be fetched.\n\n## 2. Stage the complete upstream package\n\nClone the named repository to a temporary directory and verify:\n\n- `SKILL.md` starts with YAML frontmatter\n- `name`, `author`, `homepage`, and `version` match the intended project\n- the exact upstream commit is recorded before deployment; do not mix `SKILL.md` from one revision with scripts from another\n- required sibling paths such as `scripts/`, `references/`, `assets/`, and `templates/` exist\n- the main engine or entry point named by `SKILL.md` exists\n- suspicious identifiers from the collided package are absent\n- generated validation artifacts such as `__pycache__/` and `*.pyc` are removed before copying or excluded from integrity comparison\n\nA raw-URL Hermes install may report `Files: SKILL.md` and omit required sibling files. When that happens, copy the complete verified skill directory from the same staged commit into the installed skill path. Treat installer status text and exit code as insufficient proof: package completeness is established by file comparison plus runtime loading.\n\n## 3. Back up without creating duplicate skills\n\nStore backups outside every active skill root. For example:\n\n```text\n~/backups/hermes-skills/<skill>-<timestamp>/\n```\n\nDo not place backups under `~/skills/`, `~/.hermes/skills/`, profile skill directories, or configured external skill directories. Hermes discovery may index the backup as a second skill with the same name, making `skill_view` ambiguous.\n\n## 4. Audit and verify\n\nAfter installation:\n\n1. Run `hermes skills audit <name> --deep` and distinguish expected capability findings from a different or dangerous package. Never downgrade a dangerous verdict merely because the project documentation predicts a caution verdict.\n2. Run the skill's side-effect-free `--preflight`, `--diagnose`, or equivalent smoke command when available.\n3. Compare every staged source file with the installed copy using SHA-256; require zero missing and zero mismatched files.\n4. Confirm `hermes skills list` shows one enabled entry.\n5. Load the skill with `skill_view`; ambiguity means another copy remains inside a discovery root.\n6. Confirm browser-cookie reads, credential access, writes, and optional network sources match the user's consent.\n\n## 5. Update discipline\n\nRepeat provenance inspection on every update. Registry resolution and upstream package layout can change independently. Keep the previous verified copy outside discovery roots until the new version passes audit, load, integrity, and smoke checks."
